WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(audience cheering)

2
00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:02.170
You may have noticed that there was

3
00:00:02.170 --> 00:00:04.470
a few empty seats in the studio.

4
00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:07.251
Let me explain why these seats are empty.

5
00:00:07.251 --> 00:00:10.470
We're celebrating things, New York, this week,

6
00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:12.970
including people who became essential workers

7
00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:14.650
this past year.

8
00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:15.750
That's right.

9
00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.560
I love that capes were made and put

10
00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.851
on new people, may they forever be there.

11
00:00:21.851 --> 00:00:25.260
It's true, and this past year, one group in particular

12
00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.700
that was hit especially hard during the pandemic

13
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:29.860
were waitresses.

14
00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:33.790
And, my mom was a waitress, and I could see

15
00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:36.720
how her life was so effected by tipps,

16
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.240
and, you know, it just, it gave me a lot

17
00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.010
of understanding and respect for that profession.

18
00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:45.180
And, I have a real affinity for tipping

19
00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:47.630
like a baller whenever we can.

20
00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:52.174
That is, thank you to my mom, who like taught me that.

21
00:00:52.174 --> 00:00:55.640
And everybody missed so much, you know,

22
00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:57.300
during the pandemic, especially

23
00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.150
something like a dining experience,

24
00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:01.960
which we hold so near and dear.

25
00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:04.730
The pleasure, the memories, the convenience,

26
00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:08.610
the deliciousness, and many of our waitresses

27
00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.380
dealt with unemployment and uncertainty,

28
00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.270
and we wanted to give back to them.

29
00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:16.160
So, 20 waitresses are actually about

30
00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:19.390
to walk into the studio, they have no idea

31
00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:21.852
that this segment is all about them,

32
00:01:21.852 --> 00:01:24.020
and when they walk in, I thought

33
00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:26.030
we could give them a standing ovation,

34
00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:28.580
do a little clapping, you know,

35
00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:30.240
just, this is their day.

36
00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:31.790
And we're here to honor them.

37
00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:33.824
Oh, I think I hear them coming.

38
00:01:33.824 --> 00:01:35.139
(joyful music plays)

39
00:01:35.139 --> 00:01:40.139
(audience vigorously applauding and cheering)

40
00:02:05.951 --> 00:02:08.034
Turn around! Turn around!

41
00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:14.813
They're all clapping for you!

42
00:02:16.688 --> 00:02:17.521
This whole show is actually dedicated to

43
00:02:17.521 --> 00:02:22.521
and about you guys.

44
00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.830
So, welcome to the show, welcome to your show.

45
00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.360
We want to honor you, we want to thank you

46
00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:31.020
for what you do in the world.

47
00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.770
Everything you have done, everything you've been through.

48
00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:38.050
Everything that you continue to persevere through.

49
00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:42.400
I was just telling our fellow guests here

50
00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:46.785
that my mom was a waitress when I was a kid,

51
00:02:46.785 --> 00:02:51.785
and it just forever impacted my life

52
00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:56.410
to see what tipps mean when you're a waitress.

53
00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:59.290
If there's anyone out there who misbehaves

54
00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:02.278
and doesn't treat you guys with respect,

55
00:03:02.278 --> 00:03:05.694
I would like to just meet them in a dark alley.

56
00:03:05.694 --> 00:03:06.527
(laughs)

57
00:03:06.527 --> 00:03:08.763
I really would.

58
00:03:08.763 --> 00:03:13.763
And I just think that you guys are such incredible heroes,

59
00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.771
so we wanted to say thank you, for everything that you do.

60
00:03:16.771 --> 00:03:20.854
(audience clapping and cheering)

61
00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:27.610
I mean, I'd love to hear your experience,

62
00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:30.440
thank you for listening to mine and my mom's,

63
00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:34.478
that left such an impression on me, but,

64
00:03:34.478 --> 00:03:37.500
like, what were some of the struggles

65
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:40.530
that you went through? Any stories to share?

66
00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:43.240
Yeah! So, when the pandemic started,

67
00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:46.190
I was working at this Caribbean restaurant called "Tilly's"

68
00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:48.750
and when they shut down, is really unfortunate,

69
00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:50.740
I just moved in with my partner,

70
00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:53.280
and, so, all of the sudden I had no income

71
00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:56.490
and they really had to, you know, take care of me

72
00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:58.500
and it really put a strain on our relationship,

73
00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.840
especially because she was a registered nurse

74
00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:02.710
going through the pandemic, so,

75
00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:05.490
I wasn't working and she was working over time.

76
00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:08.170
And, thankfully, when Tilly's opened back up,

77
00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:10.320
they rehired me and it really just showed me about

78
00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:13.600
adaptability and, like, rolling with the punches, you know.

79
00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:16.670
Adaptability is something that I believe

80
00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:20.260
in all my heart is one of the most important

81
00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:23.860
character traits that we can attain in this world.

82
00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:26.644
You can adapt, that is a super power.

83
00:04:26.644 --> 00:04:29.530
Becky, I was like, I was wondering,

84
00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:32.930
did you have any social anxieties going back to work?

85
00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:34.410
Like, "yay, we're all together!".

86
00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:37.997
and yet, you're like, "oh, it's so nice to see you".

87
00:04:37.997 --> 00:04:39.370
(laughs)

88
00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:43.660
At six feet, and a mask, and washing my hands fiercely,

89
00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:46.230
like, how was the psychology of coming back,

90
00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:47.350
as great as it was?

91
00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:50.600
Was there any also like, anxieties that came with it?

92
00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:52.320
Yeah, I think, you know, there's a normal part

93
00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:54.040
to have some anxiety coming back,

94
00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.436
but once we were in the space,

95
00:04:56.436 --> 00:04:59.470
the atmosphere that was built was just,

96
00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:01.460
people were excited, you know,

97
00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:03.560
we have patrons who've been coming, you know,

98
00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:07.190
for 20 years, and, you know, they know our staff.

99
00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:11.210
We had people even who checked in on our staff every week.

100
00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:13.750
And so it wasn't just, you know,

101
00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:17.686
like this throw you out there, you know, to the wolves,

102
00:05:17.686 --> 00:05:21.030
people were welcoming, people were excited to be there.

103
00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:24.489
And so, that I think eased our anxieties

104
00:05:24.489 --> 00:05:26.780
and we were all being safe.

105
00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:29.425
You know, we all made sure that, you know,

106
00:05:29.425 --> 00:05:31.430
do you feel comfortable? You know, socially distancing,

107
00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:34.460
sanitizing everything and just making sure

108
00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:36.187
that people have that sense of security.

109
00:05:36.187 --> 00:05:37.993
I went to a restaurant last night

110
00:05:37.993 --> 00:05:39.030
and the woman would not let me in,

111
00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:41.410
without my proof of vaccination

112
00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:44.870
on my Excelsior Pass, my guarantee,

113
00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:47.620
I'd keep my mask on and then my driver's license

114
00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:51.937
to confirm that, and I literally, and she's like,

115
00:05:51.937 --> 00:05:53.370
"look, I'm sorry".

116
00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:58.370
And I said, "no, thank you for going these extra steps".

117
00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:01.860
I know not everybody wants to deal with them,

118
00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.040
and I'm sorry if anybody gives you attitude

119
00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:06.696
because I'm very grateful for you having to deal

120
00:06:06.696 --> 00:06:09.060
with each step that this is taking.

121
00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:10.656
You're amazing.

122
00:06:10.656 --> 00:06:14.674
Dana, you work at Marie's Crisis.

123
00:06:14.674 --> 00:06:17.220
Oh my God! Which is like,

124
00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:19.266
has anyone here been to Marie's Crisis?

125
00:06:19.266 --> 00:06:21.210
(audience cheering)

126
00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:24.011
Thank you, and, 10 gay men in the back.

127
00:06:24.011 --> 00:06:25.217
(laughs)

128
00:06:25.217 --> 00:06:30.217
It is all about singing and being packed in together.

129
00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:35.450
It is the most beautiful energy and celebration of life.

130
00:06:35.577 --> 00:06:37.920
How is that functioning now?

131
00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.530
What's your experience been like?

132
00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:42.270
How do you go from sweating and singing

133
00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:45.010
and being packed in together like sardines,

134
00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:47.130
to the new normal?

135
00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:49.500
Well, at first when the pandemic

136
00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:52.160
and the shutdown happened, our whole staff

137
00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:54.490
decided to get together and go online,

138
00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:57.514
and sing and perform and stay connected,

139
00:06:57.514 --> 00:07:00.720
because we too have regulars who would be there every day.

140
00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:03.710
So we decided to make a Facebook group.

141
00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:05.384
They were amazing, they supported us

142
00:07:05.384 --> 00:07:09.520
and we went from 200 followers to 20,000 overnight.

143
00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:10.353
Wow.

144
00:07:10.353 --> 00:07:11.186
(applauding and cheering)

145
00:07:11.186 --> 00:07:12.019
Thanks!

146
00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:14.910
I just want to thank you for sharing

147
00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:16.430
your incredible stories with us,

148
00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:20.210
and we want to get you guys dinner on all of us.

149
00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:22.480
So we're going to give you a $200 gift card

150
00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:24.060
to some of the best restaurants in New York,

151
00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:26.330
which you're already, you know, working in

152
00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:29.110
and frequenting and our friends

153
00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:31.554
at the American Licorice Company, Red Vines!

154
00:07:31.554 --> 00:07:34.494
They want to tip you guys a thousand dollars each.

155
00:07:34.494 --> 00:07:36.344
(cheering and clapping)

156
00:07:36.344 --> 00:07:37.787
So, thank you, Red Vines! Thank you.

157
00:07:40.751 --> 00:07:42.489
Thank you Red Vines.

158
00:07:42.489 --> 00:07:46.656
(cheering and clapping continues)

159
00:07:47.865 --> 00:07:48.698
Thank you.

160
00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:00.820
Thank you!

161
00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:02.850
There's just something so much better about cash,

162
00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:05.970
you know, it's just so much more instant gratification.

163
00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:08.470
I just thank you so much for being

164
00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:13.470
such wonderful people and keep going out there

165
00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:16.759
and letting us know how we can serve you.

166
00:08:16.759 --> 00:08:19.842
(joyful music plays)

